## The payload (rule 5: source wins)

**The paper has no step-type taxonomy at all.** Deshpande 2025 p. 14 "FORWARD SIMULATION" describes the sim as *"any subset or combination of the steps detailed below"* — a flat sequence of full-state ops (growth, relaxation, division, diffusion), *"each simulation timestep consist[ing] of one cell division."* The words **quasistatic / dynamic / discrete / Lie-Trotter / hybrid / macro-step appear zero times** in the paper text. The three-phase time-scale taxonomy, the operator split, and the dt-scaled sparse-delta contract are a **library re-architecture** — recorded in `surprises:`.

Other reimplementer traps flagged: dynamic phase is *not* Gauss-Seidel (order-independent) while qs/discrete are; dt-scaling is baked into each dynamic step, not the accumulator; discrete steps are exempt from write-conflict checks (silent last-writer-wins); phase dispatch is `is`-identity on a StrEnum, so a raw `'dynamic'` string runs in no phase and never errors.

## What I did NOT establish (in the note, verbatim)
1. No oracle run — no differential evidence; I did not census which concrete steps in the running `smoke` model carry which `step_type`.
2. My "paper has no taxonomy" claim is from the paper **text** only — I did not check the original `github.com/fmottes/jax-morph` source, which could encode the distinction informally.
3. The paper's growth law (p. 15, `R_i(t+Δt)=min(R_i+ΔR, Rmax)`) adds a *fixed* per-step increment, not an explicit dt-scaled rate — whether the library's "dynamic dt increment" faithfully reproduces that is left to the growth entry.

